In this lesson we'll look at how to assign probabilities to outcomes and use them to predict what happens over many trials. When every outcome in a sample space is equally likely, like a fair coin, a fair die, or balls well mixed in a bag, the math becomes really simple. You just count the outcomes you care about, then divide by the total number of outcomes. A probability is always a number between zero and one. Zero means the event is impossible, one means it's certain, and a half means it has an even chance. Now here is the powerful part. If the probability of an event is p, and you repeat the experiment N times, you'd expect to see that event roughly N times p. This is called the expected frequency. For example, the chance of heads on a fair coin is one half, so in a hundred tosses you expect about fifty heads. The chance of rolling a six on a fair die is one sixth, so in thirty rolls you expect about five sixes. But here's the catch. Expected doesn't mean exact. Your actual count will vary, though the more trials you do, the closer your relative frequency gets to the true probability. Now let's see it in action.